          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What follow-up interval does guidance describe after starting a new blood pressure medicine?",
          "answer": "Guidance describes **about monthly reviews after starting or changing a blood pressure medicine, until the target is reached.** Once readings are stable, reviews are roughly **every three to six months.** Two hypertension documents in the corpus say this: *Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line* and *Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*. Both are labeled as major cardiology society consensus guidance, without naming the specific society.\n\nFor some medicines that act on the renin-angiotensin system, the first-line document adds a check of kidney function and potassium levels after starting.\n\nThe corpus does not show a difference between authorities in this follow-up interval itself. Both documents' \"where guidance differs\" sections are about diagnostic thresholds (the 130/80 versus 140/90 mm Hg convention) and how aggressively to treat low-risk stage 1 hypertension. Those differences are real, but they are not about follow-up timing.\n\nThe retrieved passages do not state how strong the evidence is behind the monthly-then-three-to-six-month schedule, so I cannot say how firmly it is supported.\n\nWhether this schedule fits your own situation is a question for the clinician who prescribed the medicine.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
